add('c0700','elite-ai-restraint-zero','Elite Dangerous (the human economy under the AI ban)','Elite Dangerous','The recurring human interstellar economy with prohibited self-aware AI','Living human residents across the scoped interstellar economy','A galaxy-wide ban does not make every government identical; ordinary robotics is distinct from self-aware AI.',
'''An interstellar prohibition on self-aware AI constrains robotics while human governments and corporate interests remain varied. This is a recognizable zero-variant technology-restraint comparison: limits on autonomous intelligence coexist with advanced industry and retained human roles. The guarantee best guess is weak across the broad population, with a wide interval because welfare and market access differ between places. The ban is not treated as proof that every kind of automation has disappeared.

 Human states, corporations and operators retain productive authority, giving moderate positive control weight rather than an independent AI sovereign. Variation and inequality prevent assigning full shared ownership to the entire galaxy. The modest nonworking scenario counts transport, extraction, services, administration, care and useful creation. The self-aware-AI ban is not itself a labor percentage, and piloting a ship remains work when useful or assigned. The scope deliberately compares the common restraint rather than inventing one universal constitution or assuming that an isolated commander’s career represents all residents.''',
(.7,.4,.25),([-.5,.98],[0,.9],[.03,.85]),('Varied political/market access gives a weak broad-population floor centrally.','Human governments and firms retain authority under the AI restraint.','Transport, extraction, care and services remain; a ban supplies no census.'),
['The interstellar self-aware-AI ban constrains Achilles robotics; human governments and corporate interests vary.'],['The common ban is the limited cross-polity comparison.','Automation depth and participation are estimated without equating all governments.'],'Retain the famous technology-restraint economy with broad institutional variation explicit.',regime=4)

add('c0701','nms-korvax-convergence-zero','No Man’s Sky (the Korvax Convergence)','No Man’s Sky','The functioning connected Korvax material and cognitive order','Active synthetic-person Korvax identities','Empty shells and inactive stored minds are not extra persons; the whole simulated universe is not this network.',
'''Korvax minds share a network, move between bodies and can be disconnected. Their material order concerns embodiment, components and continued cognitive access rather than a literal human leisure population. The guarantee best guess allows a partial sustaining floor within the network, qualified by disconnection and uncertain internal rights. Mind sharing is not automatically unlimited free support or proof that every member has equal authority.

 Synthetic independence from organic owners gives strongly negative control weight, while Convergence hierarchy and network dependence remain separate. The modest nonworking scenario counts research, maintenance, coordination, service and useful care between active persons. A mind moving through several shells remains one identity, and stored minds are not assumed awake. This zero variant retains the familiar networked society with useful synthetic activity visible. It is distinct from the Autophage’s salvage-based local order and from the Atlas’s substrate-level support of a separately scoped Traveller cohort. Exact allocation remains a broad best guess.''',
(.15,-.95,.25),([-.85,.95],[-1,-.55],[.02,.85]),('Network support provides a partial floor, with disconnection and uncertain rights.','Synthetic independence coexists with Convergence hierarchy.','Active identities retain research and care; shells and stored minds are not population multipliers.'),
['Korvax minds share a network, move between bodies and can be disconnected.'],['Energy, embodiment and network access form the explicit material proxy.','Internal support and participation are low-confidence estimates.'],'Retain the famous active networked synthetic society with its own access population.',regime=6,proxy=True,synthetic=True)

add('c0702','nms-autophage-salvage-zero','No Man’s Sky (Autophage communities)','No Man’s Sky','The active salvage, construction and exchange communities','Active synthetic-person Autophage residents','Scrap and unused bodies are resources rather than persons; visitor rewards are not a resident welfare census.',
'''Autophage build bodies and equipment from salvage and participate in tasks, rituals and exchanges. Their technological embodiment supplies a direct material connection, while useful fabrication and maintenance continue. The guarantee best guess is weak because resource access appears mediated by salvage and exchange rather than an established unconditional floor. Robotic existence is not equated with unlimited energy or secure replacement bodies.

 The communities operate independently of organic owners, giving negative control weight without assuming equal power among all synthetic residents. The low nonworking scenario counts salvage, fabrication, maintenance, teaching, care and useful creation. Ritual is not automatically work, but tasks serving others count even when voluntarily accepted. Body parts and game rewards are not a person census. Only active identities enter the synthetic proxy population. The point remains distinct from the more integrated Korvax network and does not inherit an imagined universal Atlas welfare law simply because the setting is simulated.''',
(.6,-.95,.2),([-.6,.98],[-1,-.5],[.02,.8]),('Salvage and exchange suggest conditional access to bodies and components.','Autophage independence from organic owners is distinct from internal equality.','Active identities retain fabrication, salvage and care; ritual alone is not necessarily work.'),
['Official update material introduces robotic Autophage communities, construction from salvage, assignments, rituals and exchanges.'],['Active identity rather than body-part count is the denominator.','Exact internal support remains an editorial estimate.'],'Retain the popular realized salvage-based machine community.',regime=6,proxy=True,synthetic=True)

add('c0703','nms-atlas-traveller-substrate','No Man’s Sky (Atlas-supported Traveller identities)','No Man’s Sky','The active simulated Traveller cohort while Atlas continues operating','Active simulated Traveller person identities, an explicit synthetic-person substrate proxy','Korvax and Autophage are separate access cohorts; unused avatars, inactive memories and hypothetical universes are not extra residents.',
'''Atlas maintains simulated worlds while its external hardware deteriorates. The scoped comparison concerns active Traveller identities whose continued experienced environment depends on that substrate, rather than assigning one internal economy to the whole universe. The guarantee best guess is weak and unstable at this level: continued existence has technical support, but failure risk and local resource dependence prevent treating simulation as a universal all-needs welfare charter.

 Atlas has decisive substrate control independently of the simulated residents, giving strongly negative control weight. Local craft and exchange still matter inside the experience; the simulation’s automatic execution does not erase those persons’ useful activity. The moderate nonworking scenario counts maintenance, services, care, research and useful creation among active Travellers. It is not calculated from server runtime or from the number of generated planets. The cohort explicitly excludes the separately scored Korvax and Autophage populations, so a background cosmology does not become three copies of the same citizens or an assumed single civilization.''',
(.55,-.98,.35),([-.75,1],[-1,-.7],[.03,.9]),('Substrate support is vulnerable and does not remove local material dependence.','Atlas has decisive substrate power over the scoped simulated identities.','Active persons’ useful internal work counts; server execution and planets are not participation.'),
['Atlas simulates universes while external hardware deteriorates.'],['Active Traveller identities are a distinct substrate-level person proxy.','Simulation is not assumed to eliminate internal scarcity or useful work.'],'Retain a scoped active Traveller substrate comparison rather than call the entire simulated universe one economy.',regime=6,zero=False,proxy=True,synthetic=True)

add('c0704','starbound-miniknog-zero','Starbound (Miniknog’s Apex order)','Starbound','The functioning Miniknog-controlled Apex society','Active biological Apex residents, an explicit humanlike-person proxy','Big Ape’s hologram is a figurehead, not evidence of an AI sovereign; stored food and machines are not citizens.',
'''The Apex inspection describes food as scarce despite advanced devices and Miniknog rule. The guarantee best guess is therefore weak: storage and technical control do not demonstrate dependable equal access to necessities. This zero variant uses a cautious interpretation of technological labor substitution alongside continuing humanlike work, without claiming that a refrigerator or powerful weapon proves fully automatic production.

 Miniknog’s concentrated biological political authority is central. Big Ape is a fictional figurehead represented by a hologram, so the boss encounter is not taken as proof that an AI owns Apex society. The modest nonworking scenario counts production, administration, technical maintenance, care and useful creation. Food scarcity does not establish unemployment or inactivity, and holographic projectors are not persons. Active Apex residents form the explicit biological proxy. The broad intervals preserve uncertainty about productive ownership and household arrangements while retaining the familiar technological dictatorship with its controller distinction corrected.''',
(.85,.03,.25),([.3,1],[0,.4],[.03,.8]),('Scarce food suggests weak ordinary secure access despite devices.','Biological Miniknog concentration is central; Big Ape’s hologram is a figurehead.','Active Apex scenario retains production and care; scarcity is not inactivity.'),
['An Apex inspection calls food scarce.','Big Ape is a Miniknog fictional figurehead represented by a hologram.'],['General labor substitution is a low-confidence technical-context qualification.','Active biological Apex residents are an explicit humanlike proxy.'],'Retain the popular technical dictatorship with its figurehead distinguished from a machine ruler.',proxy=True,extra=['src-starbound-bigape-figurehead'])

